  },
  "body": "\n<div class=\"wp-block-buttons is-layout-flex wp-block-buttons-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-button\"><a class=\"wp-block-button__link has-dark-gray-background-color has-background wp-element-button\" href=\"https://hokkienlang.wordpress.com/zh-tw-tangliengim-romanisation/\">繁體中文</a></div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-button\"><a class=\"wp-block-button__link has-bright-blue-background-color has-background wp-element-button\" href=\"https://hokkienlang.wordpress.com/zh-cn-tangliengim-romanisation/\">简体中文</a></div>\n</div>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-medium-font-size wp-block-paragraph\"><a href=\"/learn/introduction/\">Tangliengim</a> (TLI) represents Hokkien in the Latin alphabet as <strong><em>lomari</em></strong>.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-medium-font-size wp-block-paragraph\">Tangliengim’s lomari has its own spelling and five-tone system. Although it is broadly convertible from Pe̍h-ōe-jī (POJ) and Tâi-lô, some spellings differ:</p>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-table is-style-stripes has-small-font-size\"><table class=\"has-fixed-layout\"><thead><tr><th>Hangul</th><th>Lomari (TLI)</th><th>POJ</th><th>Tâi-lô</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ㄸ</td><td><strong>r</strong></td><td>j</td><td>j</td></tr><tr><td>ㅈ</td><td><strong>j</strong></td><td>ch</td><td>ts</td></tr><tr><td>ㅊ</td><td><strong>ch</strong></td><td>chh</td><td>tsh</td></tr><tr><td>ㅓ</td><td><strong>or</strong></td><td>o͘</td><td>oo</td></tr><tr><td>ㅓ+ final</td><td><strong>or + <em>final</em></strong><br>(e.g., 억 <strong>ork</strong>)</td><td>o + final<br>(e.g., ok)</td><td>o + final<br>(e.g., ok)</td></tr><tr><td>ㅓ+ㅎ</td><td><strong>orh</strong></td><td>o͘&nbsp;h</td><td>ooh</td></tr><tr><td>ㅓ+ㅇ</td><td><strong>ong</strong></td><td>ong</td><td>ong</td></tr><tr><td>final -ㄹ<br>(nasal sound)</td><td><strong>◌̰</strong><br>(e.g., 알 <strong>a̰</strong>)</td><td>ⁿ<br>(e.g., aⁿ)</td><td>nn<br>(e.g., ann)</td></tr></tbody></table><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Table 1: Spelling comparison table between Lomari (TLI), POJ and Tâi-lô</figcaption></figure>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-medium-font-size wp-block-paragraph\">For the syllables listed below, letters <strong>y</strong> and <strong>w</strong> are used at the beginning, while <strong>i</strong> and <strong>u</strong> are used after an initial consonant:</p>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-table is-style-stripes has-small-font-size\"><table class=\"has-fixed-layout\"><thead><tr><th>Hangul</th><th>Without an initial (e.g., 요 yo)</th><th>After an initial (e.g., 교 kio)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ㅑ</td><td>야 ya</td><td>-ㅑ -ia</td></tr><tr><td>ㅕ</td><td>여 yor</td><td>-ㅖ -ior</td></tr><tr><td>ㅖ</td><td>예 ye</td><td>-ㅖ -ie</td></tr><tr><td>ㅘ</td><td>와 oa*</td><td>-ㅘ -oa</td></tr><tr><td>ㅚ</td><td>외 oe*</td><td>-ㅚ -oe</td></tr><tr><td>ㅛ</td><td>요 yo</td><td>-ㅛ -io</td></tr><tr><td>ㅜ</td><td>우 u*</td><td>-ㅜ -u</td></tr><tr><td>ㅠ</td><td>유 yu</td><td>-ㅠ -iu</td></tr><tr><td>ㅞ</td><td>웨 we</td><td>-ㅞ -ue</td></tr><tr><td>ㅟ</td><td>위 wi</td><td>-ㅟ -ui</td></tr><tr><td>ㅣ</td><td>이 i*</td><td>-ㅣ -i</td></tr></tbody></table><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Table 2: Initial y and w usage</figcaption></figure>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-medium-font-size wp-block-paragraph\">* 이 and 우 remains as <em><strong>i</strong></em> and <em><strong>u</strong></em>; ㅘ and ㅚ are always written as <em><strong>oa</strong></em> and <em><strong>oe</strong></em>.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Tone marking</h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-medium-font-size wp-block-paragraph\">Tone marks follow Tangliengim’s <a href=\"/learn/introduction/#tones\">five-tone system</a> introduced below, which differ from the other Hokkien romanisation schemes.</p>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-table is-style-stripes has-small-font-size\"><table class=\"has-fixed-layout\"><thead><tr><th class=\"has-text-align-left\" data-align=\"left\">Middle Chinese names</th><th>Tone value (Taipei)</th><th>Lomari (TLI)</th><th>POJ</th><th>Tâi-lô</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"has-text-align-left\" data-align=\"left\">陰平 &#8220;dark level&#8221;</td><td>44 ˦</td><td><strong>① â</strong></td><td>① a</td><td>① a</td></tr><tr><td class=\"has-text-align-left\" data-align=\"left\">陰上 &#8220;dark rising&#8221;</td><td>53 ˥˧</td><td><strong>② à</strong></td><td>② á</td><td>② á</td></tr><tr><td class=\"has-text-align-left\" data-align=\"left\">陰去 &#8220;dark departing&#8221;</td><td>21 ˨˩</td><td><strong>③ a</strong></td><td>③ à</td><td>③ à</td></tr><tr><td class=\"has-text-align-left\" data-align=\"left\">陰入 &#8220;dark entering&#8221;</td><td>32 ˧˨</td><td><strong>③ ak/at/ap/ah</strong></td><td>④ ak/at/ap/ah</td><td>④ ak/at/ap/ah</td></tr><tr><td class=\"has-text-align-left\" data-align=\"left\">陽平 &#8220;light level&#8221;</td><td>214 ˨˩˦</td><td><strong>④ á</strong></td><td>⑤ â</td><td>⑤ â</td></tr><tr><td class=\"has-text-align-left\" data-align=\"left\">陽上 &#8220;light rising&#8221;</td><td><span style=\"color: gray\"><em>(Used only in Quanzhou, merged with 陽去)</em></span></td><td><em>⑥ ǎ</em></td><td><em>⑥ ǎ</em></td><td><em>⑥ ǎ</em></td></tr><tr><td class=\"has-text-align-left\" data-align=\"left\">陽去 &#8220;light departing&#8221;</td><td>22 ˨</td><td><strong>⑤ ā</strong></td><td>⑦ ā</td><td>⑦ ā</td></tr><tr><td class=\"has-text-align-left\" data-align=\"left\">陽入 &#8220;light entering&#8221;</td><td>4 ˦</td><td><strong>① âk/ât/âp/âh</strong></td><td>⑧ a̍k/a̍t/a̍p/a̍h</td><td>⑧ a̍k/a̍t/a̍p/a̍h</td></tr></tbody></table><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Table 3: Tone marking comparison table</figcaption></figure>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-medium-font-size wp-block-paragraph\">In connected speech, Tangliengim&#8217;s lomari <strong>writes tones in their pronounced forms</strong> after tone sandhi, rather than preserving the original citation-tone categories used by POJ and Tâi-lô. <em>Hyphens are also used to indicate tone sandhi</em>, rather than for morphemes (<em>or &#8220;what constitutes a word&#8221;</em>).<sup data-fn=\"56a12087-bad2-4d71-8eae-be01cb03eafa\" class=\"fn\"><a href=\"#56a12087-bad2-4d71-8eae-be01cb03eafa\" id=\"56a12087-bad2-4d71-8eae-be01cb03eafa-link\">1</a></sup></p>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-table has-small-font-size\"><table class=\"has-fixed-layout\"><thead><tr><th>System</th><th>Example (<span style=\"font-family:Sans-serif, Noto Sans\">대<ruby style=\"position: relative\">완<rt style=\"font-size: 120%;position: absolute;top: -1.15em;left: 0.2em;z-index: -1\">ˏ</rt></ruby></span>)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Lomari (TLI)</strong></td><td><strong>Tai-oán</strong> (<em>citation tone: tái oán</em>)</td></tr><tr><td>POJ</td><td>Tâi-oân</td></tr><tr><td>Tâi-lô</td><td>Tâi-uân</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-medium-font-size wp-block-paragraph\">The letter marked with tone is decided in the following sequence (with <em><strong>a</strong></em> having the highest priority):</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-text-align-center has-medium-font-size wp-block-paragraph\"><code>a → e → o → u → i → n → m</code></p>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Footnotes</h3>\n\n\n"
}
